From 042162a6e0438f354f7db1f7a8f505ed5b66458b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 14:02:31 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] irqchip/brcmstb-l2: set up the generic chip before chaining
 the parent

brcmstb_l2_intc_probe() installs the chained parent handler before it fills in
the data structure that handler dereferences:

	irq_set_chained_handler_and_data(parent_irq,
					 brcmstb_l2_intc_irq_handle, data);

	data->gc = irq_get_domain_generic_chip(data->domain, 0);
	data->gc->reg_base = base;
	...
	data->status_offset = init_params->cpu_status;
	data->mask_offset = init_params->cpu_mask_status;

irq_set_chained_handler_and_data() does more than record the handler. On the
is_chained path it calls irq_activate_and_startup(desc, IRQ_RESEND), which
unmasks the parent line and re-sends whatever is already pending on it. If the
parent is pending at that moment the handler runs immediately, from inside that
call, and its very first statement is

	status = irq_reg_readl(b->gc, b->status_offset) & ...

with b->gc still NULL. That is an unhandled NULL dereference in interrupt
context during an initcall, i.e. the machine dies before init runs:

  Unable to handle kernel NULL pointer dereference at virtual address 8
  pc : brcmstb_l2_intc_irq_handle+0x40/0x1a0
  Call trace:
    brcmstb_l2_intc_irq_handle
    handle_irq_desc
    generic_handle_domain_irq
    gic_handle_irq
    ...
    _raw_spin_unlock_irqrestore          (irq_set_chained_handler_and_data's own
    irq_set_chained_handler_and_data      unlock -- the handler ran inside it)
    brcmstb_l2_intc_probe.isra.0
    platform_irqchip_probe
  Kernel panic - not syncing: Oops: Fatal exception in interrupt

Observed on a Raspberry Pi 4 where the display L2 controller (aon_intr,
interrupt-controller@7ef00100) is the sole edge-triggered GIC line on the SoC,
so its parent SPI latches a pending state from the HDMI activity the firmware
performs before Linux starts. Whether the parent happens to be pending is
outside the driver's control, so the window is real on any board whose parent
line can arrive asserted.

Note that no extra masking is needed to fix this: the probe already writes
0xffffffff to cpu_mask_set (and to cpu_clear unless brcm,irq-can-wake) right
after mapping the registers, long before this point. An early spurious parent
therefore finds status == 0 and takes the existing handle_bad_irq() path
harmlessly -- provided b->gc is readable. The only defect is the ordering.

Move the chaining call down so it runs once everything the handler and the
generic chip's callbacks touch is in place. It has to stay ahead of the
enable_irq_wake() in the can_wake branch, which needs the parent started, so
just before that branch is the correct spot.

No functional change other than the ordering: the diff moves three lines and
adds a comment.

--- NOTE FOR THE INTEGRATOR (downstream, not part of the upstream submission) ---

THIS PATCH MUST BE REVERTED (deleted) ONCE UPSTREAM CARRIES THE FIX. It is a
local carry of a fix to upstream code, not a downstream feature, so keeping it
after upstream lands an equivalent change would either fail to apply or apply
twice. Check it on every kernel SRCREV bump:

    grep -n -B6 'Set the IRQ chaining logic' drivers/irqchip/irq-brcmstb-l2.c

  - if irq_set_chained_handler_and_data() still appears BEFORE the
    "data->gc = irq_get_domain_generic_chip(...)" assignment, upstream is still
    broken and this patch is still needed;
  - if it appears AFTER that assignment (or the file no longer matches this
    hunk at all), upstream has fixed it: delete this file and drop its
    file://0013-... line from
    meta-xt-common/meta-xt-driver-domain/recipes-kernel/linux/linux-raspberrypi_6.18.bbappend.

The change is a pure move, so reverting it restores the pinned upstream file
byte-for-byte.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5[1m]
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 drivers/irqchip/irq-brcmstb-l2.c | 24 ++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/irqchip/irq-brcmstb-l2.c b/drivers/irqchip/irq-brcmstb-l2.c
index 38de9d0..27b879e 100644
--- a/drivers/irqchip/irq-brcmstb-l2.c
+++ b/drivers/irqchip/irq-brcmstb-l2.c
@@ -212,10 +212,6 @@ static int brcmstb_l2_intc_probe(struct platform_device *pdev, struct device_nod
 		goto out_free_domain;
 	}
 
-	/* Set the IRQ chaining logic */
-	irq_set_chained_handler_and_data(parent_irq,
-					 brcmstb_l2_intc_irq_handle, data);
-
 	data->gc = irq_get_domain_generic_chip(data->domain, 0);
 	data->gc->reg_base = base;
 	data->gc->private = data;
@@ -244,6 +240,26 @@ static int brcmstb_l2_intc_probe(struct platform_device *pdev, struct device_nod
 	ct->chip.irq_resume = brcmstb_l2_intc_resume;
 	ct->chip.irq_pm_shutdown = brcmstb_l2_intc_shutdown;
 
+	/*
+	 * Set the IRQ chaining logic only now that everything
+	 * brcmstb_l2_intc_irq_handle() and the generic chip's callbacks touch is
+	 * in place: data->gc, its reg_base, the status/mask offsets and the
+	 * chip_types ack/mask/unmask ops.
+	 *
+	 * irq_set_chained_handler_and_data() does not merely record the handler;
+	 * for a chained handler it also calls irq_activate_and_startup() with
+	 * IRQ_RESEND, which unmasks the parent line and re-sends anything already
+	 * pending on it. If the parent fires there, the handler runs immediately,
+	 * inside this call. Doing that before data->gc is assigned dereferences
+	 * NULL in the handler's first statement and takes the machine down with
+	 * "Fatal exception in interrupt" during an initcall.
+	 *
+	 * enable_irq_wake() below needs the parent started, so this has to stay
+	 * ahead of it.
+	 */
+	irq_set_chained_handler_and_data(parent_irq,
+					 brcmstb_l2_intc_irq_handle, data);
+
 	if (data->can_wake) {
 		/* This IRQ chip can wake the system, set all child interrupts
 		 * in wake_enabled mask
